    "negative_kappa_explanation": "At least one dimension's Fleiss' kappa is negative (worse than chance-level agreement). This is NOT primarily a 'the phrasing is bad' finding -- per-rater mean scores range from 3.55 (Venky, most critical) to 4.86 (Harikha, most lenient), a 1.31-point spread on the 1-5 scale, with most individual ratings still clustered in the 3-5 (positive) range -- see overall_aggregate above, all three dimension means are above 3.8/5. This is the standard restricted-range pattern that makes chance-corrected agreement statistics like kappa unstable and prone to reading low or negative: when raters agree that something is broadly 'good,' but differ in exactly how good, the pool of scores has little spread left for 'agreement beyond chance' to be measured against, so the same underlying data that looks reasonably consistent by eye can still produce a negative kappa. The percent-agreement figures above (particularly within-1-point agreement) are the more informative statistic for this specific dataset shape -- stated here explicitly rather than letting a bare negative kappa number stand unexplained."
